[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":91},"routes/item",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10},"item",{"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":18,"_19":-5,"_20":21,"_22":23,"_24":25,"_26":27,"_28":27,"_29":30,"_31":32,"_33":34,"_35":36,"_37":38,"_39":-5,"_40":-5,"_41":42,"_43":44,"_45":46,"_47":48,"_49":50,"_51":52,"_53":36,"_54":55,"_56":-5,"_57":36,"_58":59},"expiresAt",1794444102000,"id","zti9u4dm05npwa2kbt1y5bmvg","title","AWS 安全职业经历系列第 4 部分：资深安全从业者给年轻自己的建议","originalTitle","Part 4 – Cybersecurity journeys: What I’d tell my younger self","summary","AWS 安全职业经历系列第 4 部分聚焦 11 名 AWS 安全从业者的经验教训，涵盖选择困难任务、建立信任、授权团队及硬件数据清除。Justin Mills 建议主动迎接不适、提出正确问题并挑战自己，Tyler Gabor 则通过认证从帮助台进入数据中心运维，负责测试、维修并清除服务器硬盘等介质中的残留数据。","reason","source",{"_62":90},"links",{"_59":89},"publishedAt","2026-11-11T18:21:01.000Z","discoveredAt","2026-11-11T19:17:08.301Z","timelineAt","category","blue-team","tags",[86,87,88],"score",4,"selected",false,"channel","news","story","x","readingMode","full","author","Shannon Brazil","body",{"_82":-5,"_59":83,"_84":-5,"_85":36},"outline",[64,65,66,67,68],"relatedStories",[],"topics",[60],"indexable","markdownAvailable",true,"group","hasTranslation","bodyLanguage","original",{"_61":30,"_62":63},"slug","name","蓝队与运营",{"_11":80,"_70":81,"_71":75},{"_11":78,"_70":79,"_71":75},{"_11":76,"_70":77,"_71":75},{"_11":73,"_70":74,"_71":75},{"_11":69,"_70":44,"_71":72},"sec-5","text","level",3,"sec-4","The thread",2,"sec-3","A short word from Zlata","sec-2","Pandora’s box and a pile of dust","sec-1","Choosing the harder room","zh","<p>Over the past few weeks, I’ve introduced you to people who fell into security from the most unexpected places, who work in domains you’ve probably never heard of, and who were honest about what the job is like after the excitement fades and the real work begins. This is the final post in a four-part series following 11 <span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/\">Amazon Web Services (AWS)</a></span> security professionals through the real stories of how they got here and what the work actually looks like. For this last one, I wanted to end where almost every one of my conversations ended up on its own: the things people wish they’d known sooner.</p><p>This one is about hard-won perspective, the kind you only get from doing the uncomfortable thing and coming out the other side. It’s about growth that doesn’t always feel like growth while it’s happening, and the advice these folks would hand to the earlier versions of themselves. If you’ve been with me since <span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/part-1-cybersecurity-journeys-i-didnt-plan-this/\">Part 1: I didn’t plan this</a></span>, thank you for sticking around. I hope this last set of stories gives you something to carry into your own path.</p><h2 id=\"sec-1\">Choosing the harder room</h2><p>\n <img src=\"/dailyinfo/media/58c20c835dad99d2.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"Justin Mills, Security Engineer\" width=\"400\" height=\"400\">\n </p><p>Justin Mills, Security Engineer</p><p>When I asked Justin Mills where someone should start, I expected a certification or a favorite course. His answer started with two words, “seek discomfort,” and his own career shows he means it. Justin is a security engineer with more than a decade in the field, and today he consults on how AI can strengthen security detections. Detections are the rules and models that watch a system’s activity and flag the signs of an attack, and both they and AI are changing faster than most of us can keep up with. What stuck with me was how much faster everything moved after he committed to doing the hard things: he earned two promotions in roughly 2 years.</p><p>So I asked him how, and his answer came back to the people around him. What surprised me was that he never went looking for them. Some came through luck, starting with the manager who hired him, and others arrived the way most good working relationships do: he got involved in projects, met the people on them, and the conversations just started flowing. He didn’t push or force any of it.</p><table> \n <tbody>\n  <tr> \n   <td>“Those who are meant to be part of your career story will be part of it, no shoehorning needed.” <em>—Justin Mills</em></td> \n  </tr> \n </tbody>\n</table><p>The other half of the equation was trust. Today, Justin works on hard problems, and his manager gives him room to work through them his own way, because he’s shown he can be trusted with that room. His manager even told him so directly: “I trust you to do the right thing and speak for us.” Earning that kind of trust changed how he approached the work. “That forced me to ask the right questions instead of defaulting to what I already knew,” he told me. There’s something important in that framing, because asking good questions isn’t a sign that you don’t belong; it might be the clearest sign that you do.</p><p>The lesson he had to learn later was almost the opposite of the one that got him started. Early on, growth meant taking on everything hard himself. As he moved up, it meant the reverse: learning to let go, treating delegation as a skill in its own right, and trusting the people on his team to carry work he could have gripped tightly. When I asked what he’d tell someone who feels stuck, he kept it short.</p><table> \n <tbody>\n  <tr> \n   <td>“Surround yourself with people who push you, not people who make you comfortable. Ask the right questions. Do the hard things. That’s the whole playbook.” <em>—Justin Mills</em></td> \n  </tr> \n </tbody>\n</table><h2 id=\"sec-2\">Pandora’s box and a pile of dust</h2><p>\n <img src=\"/dailyinfo/media/52bad50bd075bad6.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"Tyler Gabor, Technician\" width=\"400\" height=\"400\">\n </p><p>Tyler Gabor, Technician</p><p><span><a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/tyler-gabor-0a1243204\">Tyler Gabor</a></span> has always been the person who takes things apart. He started out selling and fixing cell phones, learning what made them tick, before moving into help desk work. From there he made his way into data center operations, where the hardware went from handheld to industrial and the stakes scaled up with it. “If it lights up or makes noise, I’m messing with it,” he told me, and he meant it.</p><p>The jump from help desk to the data center came from preparation. While he was working help desk from home, Tyler studied for a few foundational certifications, and when he could show he knew what he was talking about, he started putting his name in for roles. One of those led to AWS, though not directly: a staffing agency he partnered with placed him on a temporary assignment there, and from that point he had to prove himself all over again to earn a full-time offer. He admits it wasn’t easy and was sometimes scary, and he compares the whole stretch to the Olympics, trying out for a spot on Team Amazon alongside some of the most talented people in the field. “It was the audition of a lifetime,” he told me.</p><p>When Tyler explains his work to people outside tech, he reaches for mythology. “It’s kind of like fixing Pandora’s box,” he said, “while also making sure that Pandora doesn’t get outside of the box.” It’s a vivid image for a job that involves testing, repairing, and sanitizing server hardware so the data a machine once held can’t resurface. That can mean wiping a drive, degaussing it (using a powerful magnetic field to scramble the stored data), or physically destroying it. This is security you can physically touch, and as he put it, “It’s a little hard to get intellectual property out of a pile of dust.”</p><p>What pulled him in wasn’t a degree program or a bootcamp. It was watching high-profile leaks and breaches dominate the news as a student, which made it clear that security wasn’t an abstract discipline but something shaping real events in the real world. That curiosity never faded; it’s the same impulse that has him tinkering with hardware side projects to this day, like programmable NFC chips (the same short-range wireless technology behind tap-to-pay cards) that he brings to hacker conferences as business cards. That same urge to see what a piece of hardware is really capable of shows up in his day job, too.</p><p>One project crystallized the stakes for him. A piece of hardware came through that most people would have assumed was safe to discard, and on closer inspection it was still holding information that should never have left the building. The lesson stuck with him, and he put it the way only Tyler would.</p><table> \n <tbody>\n  <tr> \n   <td>“It doesn’t matter if the front door is locked if all the windows are open.” <em>—Tyler Gabor</em></td> \n  </tr> \n </tbody>\n</table><p>What surprised me most, though, was how much he talked about leadership, not in the org-chart sense, but the quiet kind that shows up when your team trusts you to make the right call on your own. He didn’t expect to be trusted with hardware of this caliber, and you could hear how seriously he takes that responsibility. His advice for anyone early in their career, or thinking about the switch, sounds simple but clearly took time to earn: just ask for help, because when you do, people usually give it. And if you’re already in and feeling stuck, prepare for the role you want so you’re ready when it’s time to step up to the plate.</p><table> \n <tbody>\n  <tr> \n   <td>“We are going to witness the greatest leap in human intelligence during our lifetime. As technology grows at an exponential speed, we’re going to need people to keep it in line.” <em>—Tyler Gabor</em></td> \n  </tr> \n </tbody>\n</table><h2 id=\"sec-3\">A short word from Zlata</h2><p>You might remember <span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/part-1-cybersecurity-journeys-i-didnt-plan-this/\">Zlata Pavlova</a></span> from Part 1, where she shared her path from political science to open source investigations and into protective security. I wanted to bring her back for a moment, because something she said has echoed through nearly every conversation in this series: the thing that holds people back is rarely a lack of skill, it’s the quiet suspicion that they don’t belong. Her answer to impostor syndrome is to remind yourself that you do and to build connections early. She spent years assuming her teammates already knew what she knew, and it turned out they often didn’t, because their focus was elsewhere. She also told me the biggest surprise of her current role was how much of it is writing, and especially how much of that writing means translating technical findings for readers who aren’t technical. As you move up in security, the reports, advisories, and briefs you write become one of the clearest signals of your judgment, so practicing that skill early, whether through a blog post, a conference talk, or simply explaining your work to a friend outside the field, pays off for years.</p><table> \n <tbody>\n  <tr> \n   <td>“Don’t feel embarrassed sharing something you think people already know. They might not, and you might help somebody who’s just starting out.” <em>—Zlata Pavlova</em></td> \n  </tr> \n </tbody>\n</table><h2 id=\"sec-4\">The thread</h2><p>Something I didn’t expect when I started this series was how often people’s earlier jobs turned out to be an advantage rather than a detour. Zlata told me that people who come into security from non-technical careers often explain things better, because they remember what it was like to need the explanation. Tyler’s years in telecommunications, where customers handed him phones that held their whole lives, are what first got him thinking seriously about protecting people’s information. And in Part 3, Brandon’s time administering systems and shipping software gave him the empathy for builders that his application security work depends on. The backgrounds people bring with them are part of why they’re good at this work.</p><p>If you’re early in your career and wondering whether you belong here, the people in this series would tell you that you do. If you’re mid-career and feeling stuck, pay attention to whichever story felt familiar, because that’s usually a clue about where to look next. And if this series showed you a corner of security you didn’t know existed, that corner might be a good place to start.</p><p>If you want to start, here are a few places to begin:</p><ul> \n <li><span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/training/digital/\">AWS Skill Builder</a></span> has more than 1,000 free self-paced courses, including security learning plans</li> \n <li><span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/training-and-certification/navigating-your-way-into-cloud-security-skills-roles-and-career-trajectories/\">Navigating your way into cloud security</a></span> maps the roles and skills across the field</li> \n <li>The <span><a href=\"https://niccs.cisa.gov/workforce-development/nice-framework\">NICE Workforce Framework for Cybersecurity</a></span> lays out dozens of security roles and what each one needs</li> \n <li>Your local <span><a href=\"https://defcongroups.org/\">DEF CON group</a></span> or a nearby security meetup, because community came up again and again in these conversations as the thing that speeds everything up</li> \n</ul><p>I’m grateful to everyone who sat down with me and shared their story. Hearing them changed how I think about what this field looks like, and I hope reading them does the same for you as you work out your own path.</p><p>What would you tell your younger self about getting into security? Share it on LinkedIn!</p><p>This concludes the Cybersecurity Journeys series. Read the full series: <span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/part-1-cybersecurity-journeys-i-didnt-plan-this/\">Part 1: I didn’t plan this</a></span>, <span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/part-2-cybersecurity-journeys-what-even-is-this-job/\">Part 2: What even is this job?</a></span>, <span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/part-3-cybersecurity-journeys-the-work-behind-the-work/\">Part 3: The work behind the work</a></span>, and <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/part-4-cybersecurity-journeys-what-id-tell-my-younger-self/\">Part 4: What I’d tell my younger self</a>.</p><hr><p> \n   <img src=\"/dailyinfo/media/48f4d840814f5371.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"\" width=\"120\" height=\"160\"> \n  </p><h3 id=\"sec-5\">Shannon Brazil</h3><p>Shannon is a senior security engineer, managing a team on the AWS Customer Incident Response Team (CIRT), specializing in digital forensics and cloud security investigations. Known in the community as AWSlady, she is passionate about security education and mentoring the next generation of defenders.</p>","zhKind","complete","蓝队","例行内容","AWS","https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/part-4-cybersecurity-journeys-what-id-tell-my-younger-self/","AWS Security Blog",{"_92":93,"_94":-5},"changelogVersion","2026-10-01T08:00","release"]
